# Scientific name transcribed instead of the common name

## ==Original Issue==

For biology (and some geography/taxonomy) article titles, the title is the
*common* name (`Beetle`, `Bear`, `Owl`, `Moss`, `Pine`, `Rodent`), but the lead's
first `{{IPAc-en}}`/`{{respell}}` template transcribes the *scientific*
(Latin) name that appears in parentheses next to it. The output therefore
pairs the common headword with the scientific name's pronunciation.

### Concrete examples

- `Beetle`    -> `koh-LEE-qp-tər-ə`  (order "Coleoptera")
- `Bear`      -> (family "Ursidae")
- `Owl`       -> (order "Strigiformes")
- `Moss`      -> (division "Bryophyta")
- `Pine`      -> (genus "Pinus")
- `Rodent`    -> (order "Rodentia")

## Investigation notes (agent)

Confirmed on "Beetle": lead says `...form the order Coleoptera
(/koʊliːˈɒptərə/)...`. The only IPA in the lead is for the scientific order; the
common word "beetle" itself is a plain English word and has no template. This is
the same root cause as the "wrong name" ticket: the first pronunciation
template in the lead is not necessarily scoped to the article title.

## ==Status Report: 2026-10-24 18:00 US/Pacific==

The scientific name is introduced by an editorial convention (italicized Latin
name in parentheses), so a lexical/syntactic rule ("skip a template whose
immediately preceding text is an italicized name") is *possible* but fragile and
would still miss the fact that the common name often has no transcription at
all. Deterministic fix not reliable. Left for human review; good candidate for
an LLM cleanup pass or for explicitly emitting the scientific name as a separate
entry rather than substituting.